# <%= current_page.data.title %>

## Considerations

### Adherence to the policy

There is an AI Coding policy [here](https://engineering-enablement.gds-reliability.engineering/InfoSec/ai-policy.html) which should be read and followed

### Mistakes

AI responses will often contain errors and should not be relied on to be correct - you will need to check the content
carefully before including in your application.

### Security assurance

Before using an AI Coding assistant you should speak with your security team and mitigate any concerns. AI Coding is
usually provided as a SaaS solution meaning that your content is uploaded to third party servers for inference, which
may be inappropriate.

### Sustainability

AI is a resource intensive technology. Using it when not necessary could be a waste of resources. Using appropriate models for the task is advisable.

### Best Practice

#### Sandboxing

Running AI Agents should be within a sandbox such as [https://github.com/govuk-one-login/ai-sandbox](https://github.com/govuk-one-login/ai-sandbox) to limit the blast radius of any prompt injection.

#### Skills

Using skills can make AI more efficient and lead to better outcomes. AI can help to write skills and they can be shared between and across teams.

#### Local inference

If you have a sufficiently powerful local machine you can run open-weight models locally, this can be cost effective and gives you more choice in model selection.
The [Ollama framework](https://github.com/ollama/ollama) is a way of running LLM models locally.

#### Choice of harness

There are different harnesses available just as there are different models, some currently worth mentioning are:
* [OpenHarness](https://github.com/HKUDS/OpenHarness)
* [Pi](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/tree/main/packages/coding-agent)
* [OpenCode](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/tree/v2)
